the 1990s were a golden age of  television animation but cartoons are a  business and even the most beloved shows  were sometimes canceled to make room for  more profitable programming here are the  real reasons behind a few of those  controversial cartoon cancellations  after debuting on MTV in 1993 millions  of kids and adults watched the two  giggling moronic metalheads of beavis  and butt-head determine which bands were  cool and which sucked that is when they  weren't terrorizing their neighbors  teachers and burger world customers not  everybody liked Beavis and Butthead  politicians called out the show for  encouraging anti-social behavior and one  family blamed Beavis is pyromania for  leading to a deadly house fire nothing  stuck though and beavis and butt-head  remained the world's favourite idiots  however after 200 episodes and the  feature film beavis and butt-head do  America creator Mike judge decided it  was time to end it speaking to Los  Angeles Times judge said I actually  wanted to stop a little sooner after the  second season I thought how are we gonna  do this anymore  I was completely burned out judge wound  up sticking around for seven seasons but  in 1997 he moved on to other projects  like his Fox animated series king of the  hill starring three old-timey cartoon  characters named Yakko Wakko and dot  Warner who were more or less imprisoned  in the water tower on the Warner  Brothers movie lot Animaniacs was a  cartoon quickly embraced by both  children and adult audiences alike  debuting in 1993 Animaniacs satirized  cartoon tropes while being completely  self-aware offering up innuendo laced  dialogue and biting social commentary  among the kid-friendly laughs Animaniacs  ran for five seasons and spun off its  most popular side characters Pinky and  the brain before Kids WB ended the show  in 1998 according to creator Tom Ruger  the end came due to a business decision  regarding another much cheaper kid show  a Japanese anime series with massive  commercial potential  Ruger explained Kids WB was handed  Pokemon for free and it pulled down big  numbers so then they wanted everything  for free  Animaniacs cost warner brothers too much  money to make and promote so off it went  at first glance Todd McFarlane's  animated spawn series looked like a show  for kids but spawn was based off of  McFarland's top-selling comic series  extremely dark and violent story about a  former assassin who gets killed by his  boss sent to hell and ends up working  for Satan hardly family-friendly  material you me to deal with now boo job  spawn ran for three seasons on HBO and  won an Emmy Award for outstanding  animated program the show's third season  was a bust however and HBO's animation  division was withering on the vine after  enduring numerous production  difficulties the show earned a quiet  cancellation in 1999 Batman the Animated  Series represented a breakthrough in  television animation and comic book  adaptation moody quiet and literally  dark it demonstrated that there was a  market for dramatic superhero content  that could appeal to children and adults  this Batman was so highly regarded that  Fox aired the show in primetime for a  while and in 1993 the studio released  Batman mask of the phantasm in theaters  voice actor Kevin Conroy even tops many  lists of the best Batman actors however  in 1995 after three years and 85  episodes the series left the air  according to Conroy even though the  audience was still invested and the  actors were willing to continue the  creator's had run out of ideas and  wanted to end the show on a high note  they didn't want to compromise on the  quality of what they'd had and start  creating kind of silly stories  fortunately for Batman fans the story  ideas didn't dry up for long a sequel  series to the beloved show debuted a  couple of years after its cancellation  bringing back most of the animated  series cast and crew including Conroy  the new Batman adventures debuted on  Kids WB in September of 1997 originally  created for ABC The Critic was an  animated sitcom about a snobby hapless  New York City movie reviewer named Jay  Sherman he hates most movies he sees  tries to raise his son as best as he can  and fend off his wacky family according  to Jon Lovitz who provided the voice of  Jay we did it for ABC and ABC goes we  love it and then they cancelled it after  seven shows following it's ABC  cancellation The Critic moved over to  Fox and aired immediately after The  Simpsons where creators Al Jean and Mike  Reiss had previously worked as writers  and show  according to Lovitz The Critic brought  in excellent ratings for Fox retaining  90% of the huge Simpsons audience and  yet in 1994 Fox pulled the plug despite  a solid performance an executive at the  network thought the 8:30 p.m. Sunday  timeslot could be better used by a new  show gene explained the guy who ran the  network then John metoya  just didn't like the show he preferred  to show that no one remembers called  House of buggin with John Leguizamo the  sketch comedy debuted in early 1995 in  the place of the critic and lasted a  mere 10 episodes in the fall of 1994 the  syndicated Disney Afternoon programming  block mixed things up by adding  gargoyles to its lineup of silly  cartoons and wacky adventures this  contemplative and sometimes violent show  is stylistically different from its  Disney Afternoon predecessors like goof  troop tales Minh and ducktales similar  in tone and approach to batman the  animated series gargoyles concerns the  day-to-day activities of magical beings  who protect New York City at night  the characters were architectural  gargoyles by day imbued with the spirits  of 1000 year old Scottish folk creatures  gargoyles ran for three years and 78  episodes but was ultimately cancelled  because it wasn't a mega hit according  to the creator Greg Weisman it didn't  perform up to expectations Disney really  wanted it to outdo Mighty Morphin Power  Rangers and it failed to succeed at that  angry beavers tells the story of two  beaver bachelors named AG and Norb who  live in an Oregon forest by 2001 four  seasons had aired and cancellation  seemed imminent according to co-creator  Keith Catterick we were significantly  over-budget behind schedule and had  generally worn out our welcome realizing  that the end was near Kocurek and the  rest of the angry beavers staff decided  to go out on a blaze of glory Kocurek  and show staff writer Roger ash Bakker  wrote a script for a series finale  episode called bye-bye beavers an  installment equal parts dark and meta  Dagon nor become to realize that they  are not real but cartoons and that their  show is about to be canceled which means  they will cease to exist  at first Nickelodeon signed off on the  script but when executives noticed all  the jokes making fun of Nickelodeon they  reversed course according to writer  Michael Wright they saw it all put  together and said wait this makes us  look bad so they killed it the script  was never animated but  courting of the show's voice actors  reading it was made which leaked online  in 2006 by 1995 Comedy Central was  experimenting with original programming  and one of its first new shows was a  low-budget animated series with largely  improvised dialogue called dr. Katz  professional therapist doc does it  bother you when I touch your face like  this a little bit is that inappropriate  you know probably for me to hold my hand  there nope it's not a problem  comedian Jonathan Katz voiced a  therapist whose clients were all  stand-up comedians who'd lay on his  couch and relayed neuroses that were  really just bits from their stage  routines he also had a lazy adult son  named Ben and attempted to have a social  life ironically it's dr. Katz own  success that led to its demise the show  helped put Comedy Central on the TV map  showing that the channel had a unique  sensibility and was a home for animated  TV two years after its debut South Park  premiered on Comedy Central and became a  monster hit particularly with young male  viewers dr. Katz attracted an older  audience and since it wasn't in the  demographic that the now South Park  obsessed network was trying to lure it  got cancelled with the arrival of  child-friendly cable networks like  Nickelodeon and the popularity of  syndicated shows the ratings for once  beloved Saturday morning cartoons  started to slip NBC president Brandon  Tartikoff  analyzed the budgets and found that the  average Saturday morning cartoon cost  300 thousand dollars to produce  Tartikoff and his team realized that it  would be much cheaper to get rid of the  cartoons in favor of new shows or sports  in 1991 NBC signed a long-term contract  with Notre Dame to air the college's  games on Saturday mornings it cost the  network about 1 million dollars a game  which was still cheaper than programming  six hours of cartoons every week plus it  could charge higher advertising rates  for football than it could for kids  stuff in 1992 NBC switched to a lineup  that included The Today Show  football games and a block of low-budget  sitcoms like Saved by the Bell  eliminating the entire 1991 to 1992  Saturday morning cartoon schedule check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite shows are coming soon subscribe  to our YouTube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  you  [Music]
 

